    image: "/images/belka-strelka.jpg",
    description: `Less than three years after Laika's fatal flight had proven that a living creature could survive the violence of launch, the Soviet space program set out to answer the question her mission never could: could an animal survive the entire round trip and come home alive? On August 19, 1960, a mixed-breed stray named Belka ("Squirrel"), a scrappy and excitable dog, and a calmer, camera-friendly companion named Strelka ("Little Arrow") lifted off from the Baikonur Cosmodrome aboard Korabl-Sputnik 2, a spacecraft the world came to know by its public designation, Sputnik 5. They were not traveling alone. Packed into the capsule alongside them was an entire flying menagerie assembled to study how radiation, weightlessness, and the stress of launch affected different forms of life all at once: forty black mice, two white rats, a gray rabbit, fruit flies, several plant species, and cultures of fungi and bacteria, each wired into its own miniature experiment.

Over roughly twenty-five hours and seventeen complete orbits of Earth, cameras mounted inside the capsule beamed live video back to Soviet ground stations, letting engineers—and, eventually, the public—watch Belka and Strelka floating and fidgeting in their small harnesses in real time, the first time anyone had observed living animals behaving in weightlessness as it happened rather than reconstructing it afterward from instrument readings. The truly historic moment, however, came at the end of the flight rather than during it. Where Laika's mission had never included any way to bring her home, engineers had spent the intervening years solving exactly that problem: as the capsule reentered the atmosphere, its heat shield endured the searing friction of deceleration, parachutes deployed on schedule, and Belka and Strelka touched down safely on Russian soil, wagging and disoriented but very much alive, becoming the first higher living organisms ever to survive a trip to orbit and back.

The successful recovery gave Soviet engineers exactly the confidence they needed to move forward with human spaceflight, since Belka and Strelka had proven that the thermal protection, parachute systems, and life-support technology required to bring a cosmonaut home intact from orbit actually worked—groundwork that Yuri Gagarin would ride into history less than eight months later. But the mission's most enduring legacy turned out to be far more personal than technical: Strelka later gave birth to a litter of puppies, and Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev, in an unlikely gesture of Cold War goodwill, gifted one of them, a dog named Pushinka, to President John F. Kennedy's young daughter Caroline in 1961. Pushinka went on to have puppies of her own with a Kennedy family dog named Charlie, offspring the president reportedly joked about calling "pupniks," turning the offspring of a Soviet space dog into, quite literally, a small piece of détente living in the White House.`,
