      "Yuri Gagarin became the first human to travel into space and orbit Earth.",
    image: "/images/yuri-gagarin.jpg",
    description: `At 9:07 a.m. Moscow time on April 12, 1961, a twenty-seven-year-old Soviet Air Force pilot and the son of a collective-farm carpenter climbed into a cramped, spherical descent module bolted atop a modified intercontinental ballistic missile at the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan. He had been chosen from a secret shortlist of candidates narrowed down for their compact frames, which fit the tiny Vostok capsule, and for their calm temperament under pressure. As the engines ignited beneath him, Yuri Gagarin radioed a single word that would become immortal in the history of exploration: "Poyekhali!"—"Let's go!"—and became the first human being in history to leave the pull of Earth's gravity and travel into space.

Vostok 1 carried Gagarin once around the planet in 108 minutes, reaching an altitude of roughly 203 miles and speeds near 17,500 miles per hour, all while doctors monitored his heart rate and breathing from the ground to learn, for the first time, how a human body actually responded to the crushing acceleration of launch and the eerie stillness of weightlessness. Engineers were so uncertain how a pilot's judgment might be affected by spaceflight that Gagarin's manual controls were locked before launch, accessible only through a sealed envelope containing an override code, to be opened solely in a genuine emergency. Reentry brought its own quiet drama: at roughly four miles above the ground, Gagarin ejected from the capsule as planned and parachuted down separately, landing in a farm field near the Volga River, where a startled local woman and her granddaughter, confronted by a man in a bulky orange pressure suit, reportedly backed away in fear until he explained, in plain Russian, that he had simply come from space and needed to find a telephone.

Because international aviation rules at the time required a pilot to land inside the vehicle they flew in order for a flight to be officially certified, Soviet officials concealed Gagarin's separate parachute landing for years, ensuring his flight would stand unchallenged in the record books as the first human spaceflight. Overnight, Gagarin became the most famous human being alive, mobbed by adoring crowds on goodwill tours through more than thirty countries and celebrated as a symbol of Soviet technological triumph. His flight did far more than win a single round of the Cold War's propaganda contest: it proved, conclusively, that a person could survive the entire arc of launch, orbit, and reentry, effectively firing the starting gun on the era of human spaceflight and pushing President Kennedy, just weeks later, to publicly commit the United States to reaching the Moon. Gagarin never returned to orbit; barred from risking the nation's most valuable propaganda asset on another spaceflight, he instead trained other cosmonauts until his death in 1968, when the MiG-15 training jet he was piloting crashed under circumstances that remain debated by historians to this day. April 12 is now celebrated around the world as the International Day of Human Space Flight, and informally, among space enthusiasts, as Yuri's Night.`,
