    shortDescription:
      "Alan Shepard became the first American to travel into space during a suborbital flight.",
    image: "/images/alan-shepard.jpg",
    description: `Just twenty-three days after Yuri Gagarin's orbital flight stunned the world, the United States scrambled to answer with its own milestone, one deliberately designed to be broadcast live on national television so the country could watch, in real time, whether its own space program could deliver where Soviet secrecy had not been required to. On May 5, 1961, astronaut Alan Shepard climbed into a Mercury capsule barely large enough to sit in, one he had personally christened Freedom 7, and rode a modified Redstone missile off the launch pad at Cape Canaveral.

Unlike Gagarin's full orbit, Shepard's flight was suborbital, an arcing fifteen-minute-twenty-eight-second hop that carried him to an altitude of roughly 116 miles and 302 miles downrange before his capsule splashed down in the Atlantic Ocean, where the crew of the USS Lake Champlain hoisted both astronaut and spacecraft from the water within minutes. Shepard experienced only about five minutes of true weightlessness and endured launch and reentry forces reaching nearly 11 times the pull of Earth's gravity, all while an estimated forty-five million Americans watched the launch live, a stark contrast to the Soviet Union's practice of announcing Gagarin's flight only after it had already succeeded.

Though modest next to Gagarin's achievement, the flight delivered an enormous psychological victory for a nation still stinging from being beaten into space, proving American hardware and astronaut training could perform under the glare of live, unedited television. Its success gave President Kennedy the political and technical confidence, just twenty days later, to stand before a joint session of Congress and commit the country to landing a man on the Moon before the decade was out. Shepard's own career nearly ended there: grounded for years by Ménière's disease, an inner-ear disorder that caused debilitating dizziness, he underwent experimental surgery to correct it and returned to spaceflight a full decade later as commander of Apollo 14 in 1971, becoming, at forty-seven, the oldest person to walk on the Moon up to that time and famously smuggling along a makeshift six-iron club head to hit golf balls across the lunar surface, turning the barren Fra Mauro highlands into the unlikeliest driving range in the solar system.`,
