    image: "/images/valentina-tereshkova.jpg",
    description: `On June 16, 1963, the Soviet Union sent a twenty-six-year-old former textile factory worker and amateur skydiver, with no formal piloting experience at all, into orbit aboard Vostok 6, and in doing so made Valentina Tereshkova the first woman in human history to travel into space. She had been selected from a secret pool of more than four hundred applicants and, alongside four other female trainees, put through a grueling selection process that Soviet leadership designed as much for propaganda purposes as for science: deliberately choosing a candidate from the working class, radioing under the call sign "Chaika," meaning "Seagull," to showcase Soviet claims of gender equality under communism to a watching world.

Over nearly three days and forty-eight orbits of Earth, Tereshkova logged more time in space than the combined total of every American astronaut who had flown before her, a statistic Soviet state media broadcast widely and proudly. The flight was not without difficulty: Tereshkova reportedly struggled with disorientation and motion sickness for much of the mission, and she is credited with noticing and reporting an error in the spacecraft's automatic navigation program that, if uncorrected, would have sent the capsule into a higher rather than lower orbit on reentry—a catch that may have saved the mission, though details about the flight's technical problems stayed classified for decades.

Whatever difficulties lay behind the scenes, Tereshkova's flight was an undeniable historic first, yet its aftermath revealed the limits of the propaganda triumph it represented: no Soviet woman would fly in space again for nineteen years, until Svetlana Savitskaya's 1982 mission, and no American woman would follow Tereshkova into orbit until Sally Ride two decades later, in 1983. Tereshkova herself never returned to space, instead becoming a prominent Soviet and later Russian political figure, serving for decades in various government roles. She remains, to this day, the only woman ever to have flown a solo space mission, and her flight endures as proof that, whatever the motives behind it, the Soviet space program put a woman in orbit a full generation before the United States seriously tried.`,
