    image: "/images/alexei-leonov.jpg",
    description: `On March 18, 1965, Soviet cosmonaut Alexei Leonov unlatched the outer hatch of an inflatable airlock called Volga, attached to the side of his Voskhod 2 spacecraft, and pushed himself out into the vacuum of space, tethered to the capsule by a slender 5.35-meter cord as he drifted roughly 300 miles above the Earth. For just over ten minutes, filmed by a camera mounted on the airlock, Leonov became the first human being in history to float freely outside a spacecraft, tumbling gently against the black backdrop of space in scenes that were broadcast to a stunned Soviet public within hours.

What viewers were not shown was how close the spacewalk came to ending in disaster. In the near-vacuum of space, Leonov's pressurized suit ballooned and stiffened far more than engineers had anticipated, expanding until his hands slipped out of his gloves and his feet out of his boots inside the suit, and by the time he tried to reenter the airlock feet-first as trained, he could no longer fit through the hatch. Disobeying procedure and risking dangerous decompression sickness, Leonov made the split-second decision to partially bleed pressure from his suit and squeeze back inside head-first instead, a maneuver that saved his life but left him drenched in sweat, his body temperature spiking and his heart racing at more than twice its normal rate by the time he sealed the hatch behind him.

The mission's troubles were not over. A malfunctioning automatic attitude-control system forced Leonov and his commander, Pavel Belyayev, to switch to manual reentry, sending the capsule hundreds of miles off its planned course to land in a remote, snow-covered forest in the Ural Mountains. Stranded overnight in freezing temperatures with wolves reportedly heard prowling nearby, the two cosmonauts had to wait for rescuers to ski in through the trees before a clearing could be found large enough for a helicopter, and they weren't evacuated until the following day. None of this was disclosed to the public until decades later, but the mission proved, at enormous personal risk to its crew, that human beings could survive and function outside a spacecraft, directly shaping the design of the pressurized suits and airlocks NASA would need for its own Gemini spacewalks and, ultimately, for astronauts to walk on the Moon.`,
