    shortDescription:
      "Two spacecraft successfully connected together while orbiting Earth.",
    image: "/images/first-docking.jpg",
    description: `On March 16, 1966, NASA launched an uncrewed Agena Target Vehicle into orbit, followed roughly 101 minutes later by astronauts Neil Armstrong and David Scott aboard Gemini 8, tasked with a maneuver essential to the entire architecture of a future Moon landing: two spacecraft would need to find each other, approach, and physically dock together in orbit, a capability any lunar mission would depend on to reunite a lander with its command module. After a carefully choreographed rendezvous, Armstrong guided Gemini 8's nose into the Agena's docking collar and successfully locked the two vehicles together, achieving the first-ever docking of two spacecraft in orbit.

The triumph lasted less than half an hour. Shortly after docking, the combined spacecraft began an unexpected and increasingly violent roll, and when Armstrong undocked from the Agena to investigate, believing the target vehicle was the source of the problem, the Gemini capsule alone began tumbling even faster on its own, eventually spinning at nearly one full revolution per second. As the rotation intensified, both astronauts' vision began to blur and they found themselves on the verge of losing consciousness, a life-threatening spin later traced to a short-circuited thruster on the spacecraft's own maneuvering system that had stuck open and was firing continuously without their knowledge.

With remarkably calm precision under conditions that would have overwhelmed most pilots, Armstrong shut down the malfunctioning system entirely and instead fired the spacecraft's reentry control thrusters—normally reserved for use after the mission—to stop the spin, a split-second improvisation that saved both astronauts' lives but used up the fuel reserved for reentry maneuvers, forcing mission control to order an immediate emergency splashdown in the Pacific Ocean near Okinawa, cutting the flight short after less than eleven hours instead of its planned three days. Though nearly fatal, the mission proved that orbital docking was achievable and gave NASA its first real test of a genuine in-flight emergency resolved through cool-headed human piloting rather than automation, a demonstration of composure under pressure that cemented Armstrong's reputation within the astronaut corps and, three years later, helped make him the natural choice to command the first Moon landing.`,
