    shortDescription:
      "Neil Armstrong became the first human to walk on another celestial body.",
    image: "/images/first-moonwalker.jpg",
    description: `At 10:56 p.m. Eastern time on July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong descended the ladder of the lunar module Eagle, planted his boot into the gray dust of the Sea of Tranquility, and spoke words that would be memorized by schoolchildren for generations to come—"That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind"—as an estimated 600 million people around the world, roughly a fifth of the planet's population at the time, watched the grainy black-and-white broadcast in stunned, collective silence. Getting to that moment had been far closer than the calm television footage let on: during the final descent, Eagle's guidance computer had begun blaring unfamiliar "1202" program alarms while Armstrong, realizing the automatic system was steering them toward a boulder-strewn crater, took manual control and flew the lander past the hazard by eye, ultimately touching down with only about twenty seconds of fuel remaining before mission rules would have forced an abort.

Armstrong was joined on the surface nineteen minutes later by Buzz Aldrin, while their third crewmate, Michael Collins, orbited alone above them in the command module Columbia, at times passing behind the Moon and losing all radio contact with both his crewmates and Earth, making him, for stretches of nearly fifty minutes at a time, the most isolated human being in existence. Over roughly two and a half hours on the surface, the two astronauts planted an American flag rigged with a horizontal wire to appear as though it were flapping in a nonexistent lunar breeze, unveiled a plaque reading "We came in peace for all mankind," collected nearly fifty pounds of rock and soil samples, and took a congratulatory phone call from President Richard Nixon before climbing back into Eagle for the return journey.

The landing fulfilled, with five months to spare, President Kennedy's 1961 promise to reach the Moon before the decade's end, delivering the United States a symbolic and definitive victory in the Space Race. All three astronauts were placed into a mobile quarantine facility for eighteen days upon splashdown in the Pacific, a precaution against the theoretical risk of unknown lunar pathogens that, in hindsight, turned out to be unnecessary but reflected how much about the Moon remained genuinely unknown. Beyond its symbolism, Apollo 11 left behind a technological legacy that rippled through daily life on Earth for decades afterward, from the miniaturized guidance computers that helped birth the modern microchip industry to advances in materials science, water-filtration systems, and telecommunications, ensuring that the mission's influence extended far beyond the footprints its astronauts left undisturbed in the airless lunar dust.`,
