      "The Space Shuttle became the world's first reusable spacecraft.",
    image: "/images/reusable-spacecraft.jpg",
    description: `On April 12, 1981—chosen deliberately to fall on the twentieth anniversary of Yuri Gagarin's historic flight—astronauts John Young and Robert Crippen strapped into the Space Shuttle Columbia, a radically new kind of spacecraft assembled from a winged orbiter, a massive external fuel tank, and two solid rocket boosters, and rode it into orbit on the first-ever crewed test flight of a brand-new American spacecraft, a level of risk NASA had never accepted before; every previous American spacecraft, from Mercury to Apollo, had first flown uncrewed. Unlike every capsule that came before it, the shuttle was designed not to be discarded after a single use but to glide back down through the atmosphere and land on a runway like an airplane, ready to be refueled, refitted, and launched again.

Over fifty-four hours and thirty-six orbits, Young and Crippen tested the untested orbiter's systems while engineers on the ground anxiously studied photographs for damage to its delicate heat-shield tiles, several of which had been lost during ascent, a foreshadowing of the exact vulnerability that would later doom the Columbia orbiter itself. Columbia touched down safely on a dry lakebed runway at Edwards Air Force Base in California, proving that a reusable spacecraft could survive the violence of both launch and reentry and fly again, a concept NASA hoped would dramatically cut the cost of reaching orbit.

Over the following three decades, the shuttle fleet went on to build the International Space Station piece by piece, launch and later repeatedly repair the Hubble Space Telescope, and carry astronauts and satellites into orbit across 135 total missions, becoming the workhorse of American spaceflight for a generation. That legacy was permanently shadowed by tragedy: the program lost two entire crews, Challenger during launch in January 1986 and Columbia during reentry in February 2003, disasters that forced sweeping reforms in NASA's safety culture and ultimately led to the fleet's retirement in 2011. Even so, the basic idea the shuttle pioneered—that spacecraft could be built to fly more than once—would go on to define the next generation of American spaceflight, directly inspiring the reusable rockets and capsules that companies like SpaceX now fly routinely.`,
