    image: "/images/sally-ride.jpg",
    description: `On June 18, 1983, physicist Sally Ride became the first American woman to travel into space, launching aboard Space Shuttle Challenger as a mission specialist and flight engineer with a very specific, hard-won expertise: she had helped design and test the shuttle's robotic arm, the Remote Manipulator System, making her one of the very people most qualified in the world to actually operate the hardware she was now flying to use. Ride had been selected five years earlier as part of NASA's 1978 astronaut class, nicknamed "The Thirty-Five New Guys," the first class ever to include women, after answering a newspaper ad seeking astronaut candidates while finishing her PhD in physics at Stanford.

In the run-up to launch, Ride was subjected to a barrage of questions from reporters that seem almost unbelievable in hindsight: whether spaceflight would damage her reproductive organs, whether she planned to wear a bra or makeup in orbit, whether she would cry if something went wrong on the mission, and how many tampons she would need for a week in space—an actual engineering miscalculation NASA made by supplying roughly a hundred for a six-day flight. Ride handled the scrutiny with characteristic dry humor, focusing instead on her actual job: during the six-day STS-7 mission, she used the robotic arm to deploy two communications satellites and to capture and release a free-flying scientific pallet, proving that a human operator could precisely maneuver a multi-ton piece of hardware in orbit using nothing but a joystick and a set of cameras.

Ride flew a second shuttle mission the following year and was training for a third when the Challenger disaster grounded the fleet in 1986; she went on to become the only person ever to serve on the official investigation boards for both the Challenger and Columbia disasters, and her sharp, skeptical questioning during the Challenger inquiry helped push investigators toward the faulty O-ring seals that were ultimately found to have caused the accident. After retiring from NASA, she founded Sally Ride Science to encourage girls and young women into STEM careers, and it was only after her death in 2012 that the world learned, through an obituary written by her partner of twenty-seven years, Tam O'Shaughnessy, that Ride had also quietly been the first known LGBTQ astronaut to fly in space.`,
