    shortDescription:
      "Astronauts successfully repaired a damaged satellite while working in orbit.",
    image: "/images/satellite-repair.jpg",
    description: `In April 1984, astronauts aboard Space Shuttle Challenger set out to attempt something NASA had never tried before: rather than simply writing off a broken satellite, they would try to fix one already in orbit. The target was the Solar Maximum Mission satellite, launched in 1980 to study solar flares, which had lost the use of its fine-pointing attitude-control system within months of reaching orbit, leaving a quarter-billion-dollar spacecraft tumbling slowly and uselessly through space, unable to properly aim its instruments at the sun.

Astronaut George Nelson first attempted to grab hold of the satellite using a jet-propelled backpack called the Manned Maneuvering Unit, but as he tried to dock with a fixture on the satellite's exterior, the attempt went badly wrong, sending Solar Max into an even faster and more dangerous tumble and forcing engineers on the ground to spend an anxious day remotely stabilizing the satellite's spin before a second attempt could even be considered. On the next try, astronaut Terry Hart used the shuttle's robotic arm to gently capture the now-stabilized satellite and berth it securely inside the cargo bay, where spacewalking astronauts Nelson and James van Hoften could finally get to work.

Over the course of a lengthy spacewalk, the astronauts replaced Solar Max's malfunctioning attitude-control module and swapped out a damaged instrument electronics box, then watched as the shuttle's arm released the satellite back into orbit, fully functional again and extending its scientific mission by another five years. The repair proved, for the first time, that satellites didn't have to be treated as disposable objects doomed to failure the moment something broke, a hard-won lesson that would prove essential nine years later, in 1993, when NASA sent astronauts on an even more complex and closely watched mission to fix the crippled vision of the newly launched Hubble Space Telescope.`,
