    description: `By the time Svetlana Savitskaya climbed out of the Salyut 7 space station's airlock on July 25, 1984, she was already a record-setter twice over: a champion aerobatic pilot before joining the cosmonaut corps, she had flown to Salyut 7 for the first time in 1982, becoming only the second woman ever to reach orbit, nineteen years after Valentina Tereshkova. Returning to the station in 1984 as part of a resupply crew, Savitskaya now set out to become the first woman to leave a spacecraft entirely and work outside it in the vacuum of space.

Over roughly three and a half hours outside the station, Savitskaya tested a specialized hand tool called the Universal Work Tool, capable of welding, cutting, and applying protective metal coatings in the vacuum of space, experiments meant to determine whether cosmonauts could eventually perform real construction and repair work in orbit rather than relying entirely on remote robotics or on parts pre-built and launched from the ground.

Soviet officials reportedly moved Savitskaya's spacewalk forward with unusual urgency after learning that NASA was preparing to send American astronaut Kathryn Sullivan on her own spacewalk later that same year, turning what might otherwise have unfolded as a routine technical milestone into one final, quietly contested skirmish of the Cold War space race—fought this time not over who could reach space first, but over which nation's woman would step outside a spacecraft first. Savitskaya's spacewalk secured that symbolic victory for the Soviet Union by less than three months, and her welding and coating experiments contributed directly to techniques later used in the construction and maintenance of the Mir space station and, eventually, the International Space Station itself.`,
